T33.01|An exchange quotation states how many units of one currency buy one unit of another. Always write the units before calculating appreciation, conversion or cross rates. Inverting a quote changes both the numerical level and percentage-change calculation.~~Bid and ask depend on whether the dealer buys or sells the base currency. A mid-price cross rate is only a simplified estimate before spreads and fees.|Direct quotation: Domestic units per foreign unit.;;Inverse quotation: Reciprocal price convention.;;Cross rate: Rate derived through a third currency.|Assume ₹80 per USD and USD1.10 per EUR, using hypothetical mid-rates.~~Multiply 80 × 1.10.;;Cross rate is ₹88 per EUR.;;Check units cancel through USD.~~Unit notation prevents multiplying the wrong rates.~~Executable bid/ask cross rates require the relevant sides and costs.|Write currency units.;;Distinguish bid, ask and mid.;;Check inverse and cross-rate arithmetic.
T33.02|Spot exchange settles under the market's agreed near-term convention; a forward fixes a rate today for exchange on a future date. A forward rate reflects financing and market conditions rather than being a guaranteed forecast of future spot. Settlement and credit obligations remain.~~Hedging with forwards can stabilise cash conversion while giving up benefits from favourable spot changes.|Spot: Near-term currency exchange.;;Outright forward: Future exchange at agreed terms.;;FX swap: Linked exchanges at different dates.|An exporter expects USD100,000 in three months and sells it forward at hypothetical ₹82 per USD.~~Contracted receipt is ₹82 lakh.;;If spot later is ₹85, unhedged receipt would be ₹85 lakh.;;If spot is ₹78, the forward still fixes ₹82 under its terms.~~A hedge reduces uncertainty rather than maximising hindsight revenue.~~Non-receipt creates a separate overhedge risk.|Match amount and date.;;Review settlement and collateral.;;Separate forward pricing from forecasts.
T33.03|Purchasing power parity relates exchange rates to relative price levels or inflation. Absolute PPP compares equivalent baskets; relative PPP considers changes in relative prices. Trade barriers, nontraded goods and differing baskets can prevent close short-run alignment.~~PPP can inform long-run valuation discussion but is not a precise short-term trading rule. A cheap currency by one index can remain cheap or change structurally.|Absolute PPP: Exchange rate aligned with basket prices.;;Relative PPP: Exchange movement linked to inflation differences.;;Real exchange rate: Nominal rate adjusted for relative prices.|A comparable basket costs ₹8,000 domestically and USD100 abroad.~~Absolute PPP implies ₹80 per USD.;;At market ₹90, compare the implied gap.;;Investigate basket, taxes and nontraded components before concluding mispricing.~~A basket comparison is a valuation lens.~~It does not guarantee convergence or an executable arbitrage.|Use comparable baskets.;;Distinguish long-run theory from prediction.;;Inspect structural and trade differences.
T33.04|Covered interest parity links spot, forward and comparable interest rates through a hedged investment. Uncovered parity instead concerns expected future spot without a hedge and is not a risk-free arbitrage relationship. Credit, collateral and funding frictions can affect observed covered relationships.~~For a quote in domestic currency per foreign unit, the simple one-period forward is spot times the domestic gross rate divided by the foreign gross rate under matched assumptions.|Covered parity: No-arbitrage relationship with forward hedging.;;Uncovered parity: Expected unhedged currency relationship.;;Cross-currency basis: Deviation linked to funding and market frictions.|Assume ₹80 per USD, one-year INR rate 6% and USD rate 3%, with comparable risk and no costs.~~Forward = 80 × 1.06 ÷ 1.03.;;Result is about ₹82.33 per USD.;;Check quote units before using the formula.~~Forward pricing offsets the rate difference in the idealised hedge.~~Real conventions and basis may alter the quote.|Match maturity and risk.;;State quotation convention.;;Distinguish arbitrage pricing from expected spot.
T33.05|Forward premium or discount describes a forward rate relative to spot under a stated quotation. A currency at a premium in one quote is at a discount in the inverse quote. Annualisation requires the tenor and convention.~~The premium primarily reflects forward pricing conditions, not a promise that the currency will appreciate by that amount.|Forward points: Forward minus spot.;;Premium/discount: Proportional quote difference.;;Annualised measure: Difference adjusted for tenor under a convention.|Spot is ₹80 per USD and six-month forward ₹81.~~Forward points are ₹1.;;USD forward premium is 1 ÷ 80 = 1.25% for six months.;;Simple annualised premium is 2.5%.~~The convention determines the label and annualisation.~~Future spot can be above or below ₹81.|State base currency.;;Specify tenor and annualisation.;;Avoid interpreting premium as a forecast.
T33.06|A carry trade borrows or funds in a lower-yielding currency and invests in a higher-yielding one, often leaving currency exposure open. Interest differentials can be offset or overwhelmed by exchange movements. Hedging through forwards generally changes the apparent yield advantage.~~Carry strategies can suffer sharp losses when crowded positions unwind or funding conditions tighten. Leverage magnifies these risks.|Funding currency: Source of borrowed capital.;;Investment currency: Higher-yield exposure.;;Unhedged carry: Retains exchange-rate uncertainty.|Borrow an amount equivalent to ₹100 lakh at 3% and invest in another currency at 8%; assume that currency loses 10% against funding currency.~~Investment becomes 108 × 0.90 = ₹97.2 equivalent.;;Repayment is ₹103.;;Loss is ₹5.8 before costs.~~The 5-point rate advantage did not prevent a loss.~~Exact results require quote direction and cash dates.|Calculate combined currency return.;;Stress sudden reversals.;;Assess funding, leverage and hedge cost.
T33.07|Transaction exposure arises from specific foreign-currency receipts or payments; translation exposure from converting foreign accounts for reporting; economic exposure from long-term competitive and cash-flow effects. They can overlap but require different responses.~~A translation adjustment may not immediately move cash, while a competitive shock can change future revenue without an existing foreign invoice.|Transaction: Contracted currency cash flow.;;Translation: Reporting conversion of foreign balances.;;Economic: Currency-driven change in business economics.|An importer owes USD100,000; rate rises from ₹80 to ₹88 per USD.~~Domestic payment rises from ₹80 to ₹88 lakh.;;Separate reporting effects on other foreign balances.;;Assess whether selling prices can recover the ₹8 increase.~~The invoice creates direct cash exposure.~~Long-run competitive effects require a broader forecast.|Map contracted flows.;;Separate cash and reporting effects.;;Assess pricing and competitive exposure.
T33.08|Currency hedging reduces specified uncertainty using natural offsets, forwards, options or other instruments. It should begin with a documented exposure and objective. A derivative profit can accompany business harm, and a derivative loss can accompany a successful combined hedge.~~Basis, timing and amount mismatches leave residual risk. Options provide asymmetric protection at a premium, while forwards generally create a binding exchange obligation.|Natural hedge: Offsets operating currency flows.;;Forward hedge: Fixes future exchange terms.;;Option hedge: Buys a conditional exchange right.|An importer needs USD100,000 and buys forward at ₹82; settlement spot is ₹86.~~Unhedged payment would be ₹86 lakh.;;Forward payment is ₹82 lakh.;;Combined cost is evaluated against the protected budget, not derivative gain alone.~~Hedge success follows the business objective.~~Cancelled purchases could leave an unwanted currency position.|Define exposure and budget.;;Match dates and amounts.;;Measure business-plus-hedge outcomes.
T33.09|Cross-border investing and funding add currency, tax, legal, custody and country risks to ordinary asset economics. Returns should be measured in the investor's relevant spending currency. A foreign asset gain can be offset by currency loss.~~Funding currency should be compared with cash-generation currency. Moving capital internationally may also involve restrictions or documentation.|Foreign asset return: Performance in local currency.;;Currency return: Conversion effect for the investor.;;Cross-border frictions: Tax, custody and legal costs.|A foreign asset gains 10%, while its currency falls 5% against the investor's currency.~~Combined gross factor is 1.10 × 0.95.;;Investor return is 4.5%.;;Subtract applicable costs and taxes separately.~~Local performance differs from investor-currency performance.~~The multiplication assumes consistent quote direction.|Use the spending currency.;;Inspect custody and legal access.;;Match funding currency with cash flows.
T33.10|International capital-cost estimation must align cash-flow currency, inflation and required returns. Country risk can affect cash flows and required compensation, but mechanically adding a premium everywhere can double-count risk. Company exposure may differ from headquarters location.~~Use consistent nominal or real assumptions. A USD discount rate cannot simply discount nominal INR cash flows without a coherent conversion framework.|Currency-consistent rate: Matches cash-flow units.;;Country exposure: Actual dependence on local conditions.;;Risk allocation: Places risks in cash flows or required return coherently.|A model forecasts nominal INR cash but imports a 9% USD discount rate without conversion.~~Identify the currency mismatch.;;Build a consistent INR rate or convert flows with supported assumptions.;;Review country risks already stressed in cash.~~A plausible-looking rate can produce an incoherent valuation.~~Conversion and risk premiums require explicit methodology.|Match currency and inflation.;;Map operational country exposure.;;Avoid unexamined risk double counting.
T33.11|Country and political risks include policy changes, expropriation, conflict, convertibility restrictions and institutional weakness. These can affect operations, asset rights and the ability to repatriate cash. A country's risk is not identical across all firms or contracts.~~Mitigation may include diversification, contractual protections or insurance, each with limitations. A favourable local-currency profit is insufficient if cash cannot be transferred when needed.|Political risk: Government or institutional actions affect value.;;Convertibility risk: Currency exchange is restricted.;;Transfer risk: Cross-border remittance is restricted.|A subsidiary earns ₹10 lakh equivalent profit, but permitted remittance is only ₹3 in the relevant period.~~Parent-accessible cash is ₹3.;;Track the remaining ₹7 and local uses.;;Assess restrictions and future scenarios.~~Consolidated profit does not guarantee parent liquidity.~~Actual legal restrictions require current primary sources.|Assess rights and enforceability.;;Map conversion and transfer rules.;;Separate local profit from accessible cash.
T33.12|Multinational capital budgeting evaluates project operating cash and cash available to the parent. Exchange rates, taxes, remittance restrictions, financing and subsidies can make these differ. Country-specific risks require coherent scenarios and contract analysis.~~Avoid mixing local project returns with parent cash returns. Include opportunity costs and funds that remain trapped or must be reinvested.|Project cash: Generated by local operations.;;Parent cash: Available after transfer and tax effects.;;Strategic effects: Changes elsewhere in the group's business.|A foreign project produces ₹20 lakh equivalent cash; assumed local tax is ₹4 and remittance costs ₹1.~~After local tax, cash is ₹16.;;Parent receipt is ₹15 if fully transferable.;;Add any parent tax under verified rules separately.~~Parent value follows actual accessible cash.~~Rates and charges here are hypothetical.|Model each tax and currency layer.;;Assess transfer constraints.;;Include effects on existing group operations.
T33.13|Trade finance supports payment and delivery between buyers and sellers. A letter of credit involves a bank's undertaking against compliant documents under its terms; documentary collection transmits documents with payment instructions without the same independent bank payment promise.~~Banks generally examine documents rather than guaranteeing product quality. Fraud, discrepancies and counterparty strength remain relevant.|Letter of credit: Conditional bank payment undertaking.;;Documentary collection: Bank-facilitated document/payment process.;;Open account: Seller relies largely on buyer payment.|An exporter ships ₹20 lakh goods under an LC but presents documents with a material discrepancy.~~Review the LC's exact requirements.;;Seek correction or authorised acceptance where possible.;;Do not assume shipment alone guarantees payment.~~Document compliance and physical performance are separate.~~Rights follow the instrument and applicable rules.|Match documents to terms.;;Assess bank and buyer credit.;;Track delivery, discrepancy and payment deadlines.
T33.14|Export credit and guarantees support eligible trade financing by sharing defined repayment or political risks. Cover can improve financing access but usually includes limits, exclusions, waiting periods and claims requirements. The lender or exporter retains uncovered exposure.~~A guarantee is only as useful as its eligibility, provider strength and enforceability. It should not replace commercial underwriting.|Credit insurance: Covers eligible buyer nonpayment.;;Guarantee: Promises defined payment support.;;Political-risk cover: Covers specified country events.|A ₹100 lakh receivable has an assumed 90% qualifying-loss cover and a covered default results in total loss.~~Potential insured payment is ₹90 under the simplified terms.;;Exporter retains ₹10.;;Assess waiting period, deductibles and claim conditions.~~Risk transfer is partial and conditional.~~Actual programmes have transaction-specific requirements.|Read exclusions and limits.;;Underwrite the buyer independently.;;Model uncovered loss and delayed recovery.
T33.15|Offshore foreign-currency debt may offer a lower stated interest rate but creates exchange, hedge and refinancing costs. A borrower earning domestic currency can see debt service rise when the foreign currency strengthens. Headline rates should be compared on a currency-consistent basis.~~Hedging can reduce exchange uncertainty but adds pricing and collateral considerations. Availability and regulatory permissions vary.|Currency mismatch: Revenue and debt use different units.;;Hedged borrowing cost: Funding plus hedge and fees.;;Rollover risk: Need to renew or replace offshore debt.|USD debt is 1 million; rate is 5%; INR/USD rises from hypothetical 80 to 88.~~Interest is USD50,000.;;INR interest rises from ₹40 to ₹44 lakh.;;Principal equivalent rises from ₹8 crore to ₹8.8 crore.~~Lower foreign rates can coexist with greater domestic burden.~~Compare fully hedged costs and legal eligibility.|Match cash and debt currency.;;Include hedge and fees.;;Stress depreciation and refinancing.
T33.16|Global diversification combines assets across economies, currencies and industries. It can reduce dependence on one domestic market but introduces currency and cross-border risks. International companies may already share the same global factors, limiting diversification.~~Assess correlations under stress, home-country liabilities and investor-currency returns. Geographic labels alone do not reveal economic exposure.|Geographic diversification: Different markets and jurisdictions.;;Currency diversification: Different monetary exposures.;;Economic diversification: Different underlying demand and risk drivers.|An investor holds two foreign exporters, each earning most sales from the same global industry.~~Look through headquarters locations.;;Measure shared industry and customer exposure.;;Assess additional currency and custody risks.~~Different countries do not guarantee independent returns.~~A global portfolio must still fit local spending needs.|Map underlying revenue drivers.;;Measure investor-currency risk.;;Stress common global shocks and access restrictions.
